## oneDNN

commit: `74d0475`

### Configuration

#### Scratchpad Memory

- When `ONEDNN_ENABLE_CONCURRENT_EXEC=OFF`, a global scratchpad memory is shared across primitives. This mode minimizes the amount of memory needed for scratchpads at the application level. The global scratchpad is freed when all the primitives referencing it are destroyed. In this mode, primitives can be created and executed in parallel but must be executed in the same thread they were created in. Executing primitives in a different thread than the one they were created in will result in segmentation fault.
- When `ONEDNN_ENABLE_CONCURRENT_EXEC=ON`, each primitive allocates its own private scratchpad memory. The scratchpad memory is freed when its primitive is destroyed. This mode can lead to larger memory footprint. In this mode, primitives can be created in one thread and executed in another. Also, different primitives can be run concurrently. If the same primitive is run from two different threads concurrently, the library will return incorrect results.

#### Sample

oneDNN must align with these requirements (whether enforced directly or through Bazel integration):
`DNNL_CPU_THREADING_RUNTIME=THREADPOOL`
`DNNL_CPU_RUNTIME=DPCPP` or `DNNL_CPU_RUNTIME=THREADPOOL`
`DNNL_GPU_RUNTIME=DPCPP`
`DNNL_GPU_VENDOR=INTEL`
`ONEDNN_BUILD_GRAPH=ON`
`DNNL_ENABLE_CONCURRENT_EXEC=ON`
`DNNL_EXPERIMENTAL=ON`
`DNNL_EXPERIMENTAL_UKERNEL=OFF`
`DNNL_EXPERIMENTAL_PROFILING=OFF`
`DNNL_EXPERIMENTAL_LOGGING=OFF`
`DNNL_EXPERIMENTAL_SYCL_KERNEL_COMPILER=OFF`
`DNNL_ENABLE_PRIMITIVE=ALL`
`DNNL_ENABLE_PRIMITIVE_CACHE=ON`
`DNNL_ENABLE_PRIMITIVE_CPU_ISA=AVX2`
`DNNL_ENABLE_PRIMITIVE_GPU_ISA=XELP`
`ONEDNN_ENABLE_GEMM_KERNELS_ISA=AVX2`

#### oneDNN Reference

A known-good built state of the configuration can be inspected for reference.

Reference build:
* `~/workspace/oneDNN/build_3113/`
Reference `dnnl_config.h`
* `~/workspace/oneDNN/build_392/include/oneapi/dnnl/dnnl_config.h`

Validate the project-generated `dnnl_config.h` config against the reference (known good baseline). For oneDNN related building issues take the reference build to lookup correct wiring.

To be reflected in generated `dnnl_config.h`:

`#define DNNL_CPU_THREADING_RUNTIME DNNL_RUNTIME_THREADPOOL`
